_ck._Nv="";_ck._Ov="";_ck._Pv=0;_ck._6q=0.1;_ck._Qv=120;_ck._Rv=_kn();_ln(_ck._Rv,"Descending aorta","• DESCENDING AORTA\n\n• Major artery carrying blood from heart to body\n• Diameter: 20-25mm[/color]\n• Supplies: Lower body, abdominal organs\n• Clinical: Common site for aneurysms\n");_ln(_ck._Rv,"Brachiocephalic\ntrunk","BRACHIOCEPHALIC TRUNK\n\n• Origin: First branch of aortic arch\n• Termination: Bifurcates into R common carotid & R subclavian\n• Course: Ascends slightly to the right of trachea in superior mediastinum\n• Supplies: R side of head & neck, R upper limb\n• Branches: R common carotid, R subclavian\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Trauma -> compromise cerebral/upper limb perfusion, 2) May be compressed in mediastinal masses, 3) Potential site for central venous catheter misplacement");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Coronary a.\n(right)","RIGHT CORONARY ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Right aortic sinus of ascending aorta\n• Termination: Branches into the posterior descending artery at the level of the crux\n• Course: Descends inferolaterally along the right atrioventricular groove (between the pulmonary trunk and the right atrium)\n• Supplies: R atrium and ventricle, part of L ventricle, interatrial and interventricular septa\n• Branches: Anterior branches - Conal, sinuatrial nodal, anterior atrial and anterior ventricular. Marginal branches - Right marginal artery and lateral atrial branch. Inferior - Atrioventricular, inferior interventricular, interventricular septal and inferolateral\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Occlusion -> Infarction of inferior wall of left ventricle, 2) 'Coronary dominance' determines which coronary artery gives rise to inferior interventricular artery. Most commonly RCA = 'right dominant' coronary artery pattern");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Coronary a.\n(left)","LEFT CORONARY ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Left aortic sinus of ascending aorta\n• Termination: Bifurcates into L anterior descending (LAD) and L circumflex\n• Course: Descends between pulmonary trunk and left atrial appendage into the superior aspect of the anterior interventricular groove, where it bifurcates\n• Supplies: L atrium and ventricle, part of R ventricle and anterior ⅔ interventricular septa including atrioventricular bundle\n• Branches: L anterior descending artery, L circumflex artery\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Occlusion -> Poor prognosis due to supply of L ventricle, 2) Anatomical variation of cardiac arteries is common, e.g. branches of LCA can arise directly from ascending aorta");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Common carotid\na. (right)","RIGHT COMMON CAROTID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Brachiocephalic trunk, posterior to right sternoclavicular joint\n• Termination: Bifurcates into R internal and R external carotid arteries at the level of the thyroid cartilage (between C3 and C4)\n• Course: Ascends the neck, posterior to the sternocleidomastoid muscle, lateral to the trachea and oesophagus within the carotid sheath, alongside the internal jugular vein and vagus nerve\n• Supplies: Head and neck region\n• Branches: External carotid artery, internal carotid artery\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Pulse -> carotid pulse is felt by palpating the common carotid on either side of the neck, 2) Stenosis -> common carotid is a common site for atherosclerosis and stenosis, leading to an increased risk of TIA or stroke");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Common carotid\na. (left)","LEFT COMMON CAROTID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Aortic arch\n• Termination: Bifurcates into L internal and L external carotid arteries at the level of the thyroid cartilage (between C3 and C4)\n• Course: Ascends the neck, posterior to the sternocleidomastoid muscle, lateral to the trachea and oesophagus within the carotid sheath, alongside the internal jugular vein and vagus nerve\n• Supplies: Head and neck region\n• Branches: External carotid artery, internal carotid artery\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Pulse -> carotid pulse is felt by palpating the common carotid on either side of the neck, 2) Stenosis -> common carotid is a common site for atherosclerosis and stenosis, leading to an increased risk of TIA or stroke");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Subclavian a.\n(right)","RIGHT SUBCLAVIAN ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Brachiocephalic trunk\n• Termination: At the border of the first rib, becomes the axillary artery\n• Course: Courses laterally between the anterior and middle scalene muscles\n• Supplies: Bilateral upper extremities with contributions to head and neck\n• Branches: Axillary artery, internal thoracic artery, vertebral artery, costocervical trunk, thyrocervical trunk, dorsal scapular artery\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Aberrant subclavian -> right subclavian originates from aorta (instead of the brachiocephalic), distal to the left subclavian artery. 2) Thoracic outlet syndrome -> stenosis of the middle or distal ends of the artery, leading to impeded flow to distal tributaries. 3) Subclavian steal syndrome -> primary stenosis of an artery leading to retrograde flow down the opposite subclavian artery from the vertebral arteries");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Subclavian a.\n(left)","LEFT SUBCLAVIAN ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Aortic arch\n• Termination: At the border of the first rib, becomes the axillary artery\n• Course: Courses laterally between the anterior and middle scalene muscles\n• Supplies: Bilateral upper extremities with contributions to head and neck\n• Branches: Axillary artery, internal thoracic artery, vertebral artery, costocervical trunk, thyrocervical trunk, dorsal scapular artery\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Thoracic outlet syndrome -> stenosis of the middle or distal ends of the artery, leading to impeded flow to distal tributaries. 2) Subclavian steal syndrome -> primary stenosis of an artery leading to retrograde flow down the opposite subclavian artery from the vertebral arteries");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Vertebral a.\n(right)","RIGHT VERTEBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Subclavian artery\n• Termination: Merges with the L vertebral artery to form the basilar artery\n• Course: Four segments that extend superiorly and posteriorly through the neck - preforaminal, foraminal, extradural and intradural\n• Supplies: Upper part of the spinal cord, brainstem, cerebellum and posterior part of the brain\n• Branches: Anterior spinal artery, posterior inferior cerebellar artery, meningeal branches, medullary arteries, basilar artery\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Stroke -> ischaemic or haemorrhagic insults to the vertebral arteries cause damage primarily to the brainstem and thus dysfunctional cranial nerves. Common syndromes include Weber and Wallenberg syndrome");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Vertebral a.\n(left)","LEFT VERTEBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Subclavian artery\n• Termination: Merges with the R vertebral artery to form the basilar artery\n• Course: Four segments that extend superiorly and posteriorly through the neck - preforaminal, foraminal, extradural and intradural\n• Supplies: Upper part of the spinal cord, brainstem, cerebellum and posterior part of the brain\n• Branches: Anterior spinal artery, posterior inferior cerebellar artery, meningeal branches, medullary arteries, basilar artery\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Stroke -> ischaemic or haemorrhagic insults to the vertebral arteries cause damage primarily to the brainstem and thus dysfunctional cranial nerves. Common syndromes include Weber and Wallenberg syndrome");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Internal carotid\na. (right)","RIGHT INTERNAL CAROTID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Right carotid bifurcation (between C3-C4 and C4-C5)\n• Termination: Bifurcates into two terminal branches; anterior cerebral artery and middle cerebral artery\n• Course: Multiple segments, often classified using Cincinnati classification - C1 – Cervical Segment, C2 – Petrous Segment, C3 – Lacerum Segment, C4 – Cavernous Segment, C5 – Clinoid Segment, C6 – Ophthalmic (Supraclinoid) Segment, C7 – Communicating (Terminal) Segment\n• Supplies: Anterior circulation of the brain, and additionally branches to the eyes, forehead and nose\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Carotid sinus/body ->  close to carotid bifurcation, which act as a baroreceptor for blood pressure changes, and a chemoreceptor for acid-base changes, respectively. 2) Trauma -> due to its entry into the skull via the carotid canal, a basal skull fracture can easily tear the internal carotid artery and result in an AV fistula and cavernous sinus");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"External carotid\na. (right)","RIGHT EXTERNAL CAROTID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Right carotid bifurcation (between C3-C4 and C4-C5)\n• Termination: Bifurcates into maxillary and superficial temporal arteries\n• Course: Ascends from carotid bifurcation, passes medial to neck of mandible\n• Supplies: Face, scalp, neck structures, oral cavity, nasal cavity\n• Branches: Eight branches - Superior thyroid, Ascending pharyngeal, Lingual, Facial, Occipital, Posterior auricular, Maxillary, Superficial temporal (mnemonic: Some Anatomists Like Freaking Out Poor Medical Students)\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Surgical landmark -> Has multiple branches unlike ICA which has none in neck, 2) Collateral circulation -> Important backup blood supply if ICA compromised, 3) Bleeding control -> Knowledge of branches essential for facial trauma management");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"External carotid\na. (left)","LEFT EXTERNAL CAROTID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Left carotid bifurcation (between C3-C4 and C4-C5)\n• Termination: Bifurcates into maxillary and superficial temporal arteries\n• Course: Ascends from carotid bifurcation, passes medial to neck of mandible\n• Supplies: Face, scalp, neck structures, oral cavity, nasal cavity\n• Branches: Eight branches - Superior thyroid, Ascending pharyngeal, Lingual, Facial, Occipital, Posterior auricular, Maxillary, Superficial temporal (mnemonic: Some Anatomists Like Freaking Out Poor Medical Students)\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Surgical landmark -> Has multiple branches unlike ICA which has none in neck, 2) Collateral circulation -> Important backup blood supply if ICA compromised, 3) Bleeding control -> Knowledge of branches essential for facial trauma management");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Internal carotid\na. (left)","LEFT INTERNAL CAROTID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Left carotid bifurcation (between C3-C4 and C4-C5)\n• Termination: Bifurcates into two terminal branches; anterior cerebral artery and middle cerebral artery\n• Course: Multiple segments, often classified using Cincinnati classification - C1 – Cervical Segment, C2 – Petrous Segment, C3 – Lacerum Segment, C4 – Cavernous Segment, C5 – Clinoid Segment, C6 – Ophthalmic (Supraclinoid) Segment, C7 – Communicating (Terminal) Segment\n• Supplies: Anterior circulation of the brain, and additionally branches to the eyes, forehead and nose\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Carotid sinus/body -> close to carotid bifurcation , which act as a baroreceptor for blood pressure changes/chemoreceptor for acid-base changes, respectively. 2) Trauma -> due to its entry into the skull via the carotid canal, a basal skull fracture can easily tear the internal carotid artery and result in an AV fistula and cavernous sinus");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Middle cerebral\na. (left)","LEFT MIDDLE CEREBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of left internal carotid artery\n• Termination: Cortical branches on lateral surface of cerebral hemisphere\n• Course: Travels laterally through lateral sulcus (Sylvian fissure), divided into M1-M4 segments\n• Supplies: Lateral frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes; basal ganglia; internal capsule\n• Branches: Lenticulostriate arteries, superior and inferior divisions\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Most common stroke location, 2) Dominant hemisphere occlusion causes aphasia, 3) Classic stroke symptoms: contralateral hemiparesis, hemianopia, neglect");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Middle cerebral\na. (right)","RIGHT MIDDLE CEREBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of right internal carotid artery\n• Termination: Cortical branches on lateral surface of cerebral hemisphere\n• Course: Travels laterally through lateral sulcus (Sylvian fissure), divided into M1-M4 segments\n• Supplies: Lateral frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes; basal ganglia; internal capsule\n• Branches: Lenticulostriate arteries, superior and inferior divisions\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Most common stroke location, 2) Non-dominant hemisphere occlusion causes neglect, 3) Classic stroke symptoms: contralateral hemiparesis, hemianopia");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Anterior cerebral\na. (left)","LEFT ANTERIOR CEREBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of left internal carotid artery\n• Termination: Cortical branches on medial surface of cerebral hemisphere\n• Course: Travels medially above optic nerve, connected by anterior communicating artery\n• Supplies: Medial frontal and parietal lobes, leg area of motor cortex\n• Branches: Anterior communicating, frontopolar, callosomarginal, pericallosal arteries\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Stroke causes leg weakness > arm weakness, 2) Personality changes, 3) Urinary incontinence, 4) Rare stroke location (2-3% of strokes)");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Anterior cerebral\na. (right)","RIGHT ANTERIOR CEREBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of right internal carotid artery\n• Termination: Cortical branches on medial surface of cerebral hemisphere\n• Course: Travels medially above optic nerve, connected by anterior communicating artery\n• Supplies: Medial frontal and parietal lobes, leg area of motor cortex\n• Branches: Anterior communicating, frontopolar, callosomarginal, pericallosal arteries\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Stroke causes leg weakness > arm weakness, 2) Personality changes, 3) Urinary incontinence, 4) Rare stroke location (2-3% of strokes)");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Posterior cerebral\na. (left)","LEFT POSTERIOR CEREBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of basilar artery\n• Termination: Cortical branches on occipital lobe\n• Course: Curves around midbrain, travels posteriorly to occipital lobe\n• Supplies: Occipital lobe, medial temporal lobe, thalamus, midbrain\n• Branches: Posterior communicating, thalamogeniculate, calcarine arteries\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Stroke causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia, 2) Memory problems if temporal lobe involved, 3) Cortical blindness if bilateral");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Posterior cerebral\na. (right)","RIGHT POSTERIOR CEREBRAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of basilar artery\n• Termination: Cortical branches on occipital lobe\n• Course: Curves around midbrain, travels posteriorly to occipital lobe\n• Supplies: Occipital lobe, medial temporal lobe, thalamus, midbrain\n• Branches: Posterior communicating, thalamogeniculate, calcarine arteries\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Stroke causes contralateral homonymous hemianopia, 2) Memory problems if temporal lobe involved, 3) Cortical blindness if bilateral");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Basilar artery","BASILAR ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Confluence of left and right vertebral arteries\n• Termination: Bifurcates into left and right posterior cerebral arteries\n• Course: Ascends along ventral surface of brainstem (pons)\n• Supplies: Brainstem (pons and midbrain), cerebellum, inner ear\n• Branches: Pontine arteries, superior cerebellar arteries, posterior cerebral arteries\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Occlusion causes locked-in syndrome, 2) 'Top of basilar' syndrome affects consciousness and vision, 3) High mortality stroke location");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Superior thyroid\na. (right)","SUPERIOR THYROID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: First branch of external carotid artery\n• Termination: Multiple branches within thyroid gland\n• Course: Descends to upper pole of thyroid gland\n• Supplies: Thyroid gland, larynx, sternocleidomastoid muscle\n• Branches: Infrahyoid, sternocleidomastoid, superior laryngeal, cricothyroid branches\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Must be ligated during thyroidectomy, 2) Source of bleeding in thyroid surgery, 3) Anatomical landmark in neck surgery");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Superior thyroid\na. (left)","SUPERIOR THYROID ARTERY\n\n• Origin: First branch of external carotid artery\n• Termination: Multiple branches within thyroid gland\n• Course: Descends to upper pole of thyroid gland\n• Supplies: Thyroid gland, larynx, sternocleidomastoid muscle\n• Branches: Infrahyoid, sternocleidomastoid, superior laryngeal, cricothyroid branches\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Must be ligated during thyroidectomy, 2) Source of bleeding in thyroid surgery, 3) Anatomical landmark in neck surgery");
_ln(_ck._Rv,"Facial a. (right)","FACIAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: External carotid artery\n• Termination: Angular artery at medial canthus of eye\n• Course: Winds around mandible, ascends on face\n• Supplies: Muscles of facial expression, lips, nose, submandibular gland\n• Branches: Ascending palatine, tonsillar, submental, inferior labial, superior labial, lateral nasal\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Facial pulse palpation point, 2) Facial bleeding control, 3) Facial reconstruction surgery landmark");_ln(_ck._Rv,
"Facial a. (left)","FACIAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: External carotid artery\n• Termination: Angular artery at medial canthus of eye\n• Course: Winds around mandible, ascends on face\n• Supplies: Muscles of facial expression, lips, nose, submandibular gland\n• Branches: Ascending palatine, tonsillar, submental, inferior labial, superior labial, lateral nasal\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Facial pulse palpation point, 2) Facial bleeding control, 3) Facial reconstruction surgery landmark");_ln(_ck._Rv,"Superficial temporal\na. (right)",
"SUPERFICIAL TEMPORAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of external carotid artery\n• Termination: Frontal and parietal branches\n• Course: Ascends over temporal region anterior to ear\n• Supplies: Temporal region, scalp, temporalis muscle\n• Branches: Frontal, parietal, zygomatico-orbital, middle temporal\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Temporal arteritis (giant cell arteritis) biopsy site, 2) Pulse palpation point, 3) Temporal headaches");_ln(_ck._Rv,"Superficial temporal\na. (left)","SUPERFICIAL TEMPORAL ARTERY\n\n• Origin: Terminal branch of external carotid artery\n• Termination: Frontal and parietal branches\n• Course: Ascends over temporal region anterior to ear\n• Supplies: Temporal region, scalp, temporalis muscle\n• Branches: Frontal, parietal, zygomatico-orbital, middle temporal\n• Clinical Correlates: 1) Temporal arteritis (giant cell arteritis) biopsy site, 2) Pulse palpation point, 3) Temporal headaches");
